  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 313 AM MST Thu Nov 5 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAn approaching Pacific trough will displace the ridge aloft to \nthe east and bring increased winds and even some spotty mostly \nmountain rain and snow showers later today. This system will \ngenerally be moisture starved and will offer little to no \nprecipitation for most locations. H700 winds don\u2019t look overly \nimpressive, generally in the 30 to 40 kt range, but this will \nbring a definitive shift towards breezy to windy conditions, \nespecially following the light wind regime of the last several \ndays. \n\nRidging aloft then builds back in for the first half of the \nweekend, but a trough passing to our north will tighten the \npressure gradient along the Canadian border. Forecast soundings \nhave been steadily becoming more bullish in regards to mountain \nwave activity bringing bouts of strong winds along the Northern \nRocky Mountain Front and adjacent plains Friday into Saturday, \nwith localized wind gusts between 60 and 80 mph. Winds don\u2019t look \nquite as strong for the plains east of the highway 89 corridor, \nalthough gusts between 30 and 50 mph will be common. \n\nA slightly deeper trough moves in Sunday into Monday and brings \nanother round of winds and some more light mountain precipitation,\nmostly along the northern divide. This system will have a broader\nwind shield with H700 peaking at well over 60 kts; however, there\nmay be more reliance on diurnal mixing for surface transfer \nmomentum due to less ensemble confidence in more robust mountain \nwaves. The winds certainly look to cover more real estate, but the\ntop winds may not be as strong as the previous system if mountain\nwaves are weaker and less frequent. The progressive westerly flow\npattern continues well into next week with another peak in winds \nheading towards the mid- week period. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\n...Stronger westerly winds Friday into Saturday and again on \nSunday...\n\nNBM probabilistic guidance continues to highlight Friday into \nSaturday for the strongest winds, with the chance for 55 mph + \nwind gusts running roughly 70 to 90% along the Rocky Mountain \nFront and the plains along and west of highway 89 and along and \nwest of I15 over Glacier County. The 40 to 70% chance for 75 mph \nwind gusts is starting to expand beyond immediate eastern slopes \nof the Northern Rocky Mountain Front given better agreement on \ntiming and stronger mountain wave activity. The confounding factor\nis the increasingly northwesterly component with height, which \nmay preclude wind speed optimization, mostly during the day on \nFriday. This was not enough to negate the decision to issue a high\nwind watch for the Northern Rocky Mountain Front zones and \neastern Glacier County given the overall favorable trends. Note \nthat the plains east of I15 also look windy during this time \ndespite not being strong enough to warrant wind product issuance \nat this time. Wind gusts in the 30 to 50 mph gusts will be common \nfor these locations, especially the central MT highway 87/200 \ncorridor. \n\nConfidence for another round of stronger winds Sunday into Monday\nhas gained traction over the last 24 hours. Ensembles are in \nbetter agreement with a broader 50 to 70 kt H700/H500 jet streak \nmoving through the Northern Rockies during this time. More precise\ndetails in regards to mountain waves and diurnal mixing still \nneed to be sorted out, but NBM probabilistic guidance is \nincreasing, with up to a 90% chance for 55 mph gusts or higher \nalong the Rocky Mountain Front and a 20 to 40% chance for 55 mph \ngusts or greater along the central MT highway 87/200 corridor and \nalong portions of the Hi-Line. - RCG",
